  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 241 AM MDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\n2:30 AM upper level water vapor imagery shows largely zonal flow \nwith an embedded shortwave disturbance over Wyoming, thanks to an \nupper high centered over New Mexico. This disturbance has been the \nfocus for showers and a few thunderstorms across southern Wyoming \nlate Sunday night/early Monday morning. As this shortwave progresses \neastward through sunrise, coverage is expected to diminish with only \na few isolated showers across Sweetwater County. Temperatures today, \nand most of this week, are forecast to have highs in the 70s and 80s \nand lows in the 40s and 50s.\n\nAn active day is in store as another shortwave rounds the stagnant \nupper high to our south. This shortwave looks to shift into western \nWyoming around noon/1PM Monday, allowing convection to take \nadvantage of peak heating (BL CAPE ranging from 500 to 1000 J/kg). \nMost locations have a 20% to 50% chance of a shower or thunderstorm \nthis afternoon and evening due to abundant available moisture in the \nmid levels of the atmosphere. Model forecast soundings are showing a \nclassic inverted-V profile and indicating DCAPE values around 1100 \nJ/kg. Additionally, dew point depressions range from 45 to 55 \ndegrees. These parameters suggest that thunderstorms will be capable \nof 45 to 60 mph gusty outflows and this correlates well with the SPC \nDay 1 Marginal Risk across much of Wyoming. Thunderstorms will also \nbe capable of frequent lightning and the potential for small hail \ncannot be ruled out with steep mid level lapse rates. Showers and \nthunderstorms are forecast roughly from Noon through at least 1AM \nTuesday, with the severe thunderstorm potential occurring between 1PM \nand 9PM.\n\nGiven today's shortwave will be working with monsoonal moisture and \nPWATs around the 90th percentile of climatology, any shower or \nthunderstorm will be capable of heavy rain and localized flooding. \nIt is important to note that storms will be moving today with cloud \nlayer shear in the 30 to 40kt range. The localized flooding issues \narise if multiple storms traverse over the same area. The current \nforecasted QPF for today and tonight is a broad brushed 0.01 - 0.1 \ninches due to the overall scattered, but widespread coverage, nature \nof storms today. It is very difficult, if not impossible, to pin \npoint the exact locations of showers and thunderstorms that could \ntraverse over the same area and/or produce heavier rainfall rates.\n\nOn Tuesday, the aforementioned upper high will build northwards into \nthe Great Basin with a ridge axis over Idaho. As this builds, \nanother shortwave, although weaker than today, is forecast to \ntraverse across Wyoming and bring additional shower and thunderstorm \nchances. Flow will be more west to west-northwesterly with the \nbuilding ridge to our west, so monsoonal moisture is forecast to be \ngreatest across west, northwest, and northern Wyoming. Given the \nweaker support aloft with overall height rises, showers and \nthunderstorms on Tuesday afternoon are forecast to be largely \norographically and diurnally driven along and north of the \nContinental Divide.\n\nThe upper ridge slowly shifts east across Wyoming on Wednesday and \nThursday with shortwave disturbances both afternoons. Wednesday \ncurrently looks to have less coverage and be largely terrain driven \ncompared to Thursday. This is most likely due to height rises aloft \nsuppressing more numerous convective activity. As the forecast \ncurrently stands, a more potent shortwave will be present as the \nridge shifts east out of Wyoming on Thursday. This means that \nThursday could be an active weather day for the area. Wyoming is \ncurrently forecast to be in southwest flow behind the ridge Friday \nthrough at least Sunday with embedded shortwaves each day. This \nultimately means that another weekend with afternoon showers and \nthunderstorms is looking possible.",
